---
import InfoPage from "../layouts/InfoPage.astro";

const title = "About RNG Chords | Jonathan R. Reed";
const description =
	"RNG Chords is a free chord progression generator by Jonathan R. Reed. Dice pick the chords, and you decide which ones stay.";
const published = "2026-04-21";
const modified = "2026-10-22";
const author = {
	"@type": "Person",
	name: "Jonathan R. Reed",
	url: "https://jonathanrreed.com",
};
const structuredData = {
	"@context": "https://schema.org",
	"@type": "AboutPage",
	name: "About RNG Chords",
	description,
	url: new URL("/about/", Astro.site ?? "https://rngchords.jonathanrreed.com").toString(),
	datePublished: published,
	dateModified: modified,
	author,
	mainEntity: {
		"@type": "WebSite",
		"@id": "https://rngchords.jonathanrreed.com/#website",
		name: "RNG Chords",
		description,
		isAccessibleForFree: true,
		url: "https://rngchords.jonathanrreed.com/",
		creator: author,
	},
};
---

<InfoPage
	title={title}
	description={description}
	path="/about/"
	heading="Random chords for getting unstuck"
	jsonLd={[structuredData]}
	dateLabel="Published"
	date={published}
>
	<p>
		RNG Chords is a free chord progression generator that runs in your browser.
		Roll dice, listen, and keep what you like. It works for any instrument,
		since a chord is the same set of notes wherever you play it.
	</p>
	<p>
		Some rolls are wrong in a useful way. Hearing a chord you'd never have
		written often tells you what you wanted in that slot. It also works as a
		practice prompt: reroll until you land in a key you rarely play in, then
		work the progression out by ear.
	</p>

	<h2>How the chords are picked</h2>
	<p>Every roll starts with dice. There are three ways to use them.</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Easy</strong> turns each die into one chord. A die's size, from d4
			up to d20, sets how far that chord can wander from the key.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Advanced</strong> uses five dice, one each for the root, quality,
			extensions, inversion, and rhythm.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Manual</strong> skips the dice. Type something like
			<code>Cmaj9, Am11, D7b9, G13</code> or build a chord from parts.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h3>The key</h3>
	<p>
		The dice total picks the key from nine centers: C, G, D, A, E, F, B&#9837;,
		E&#9837;, and A&#9837;. Every root comes from that key's major scale and the
		quality is rolled separately, so a minor chord can land where a major one
		"should" be. You can also pin the key yourself and every roll will land in it.
	</p>

	<h3>Chord style</h3>
	<p>This changes what the dice can pick from. It doesn't change the key.</p>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Simple</strong> uses major, minor, and dominant chords with at
			most a 6, 7, or maj7 on top.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Color</strong> adds sus4, diminished, and augmented chords, plus
			9ths, 11ths, 13ths, and altered notes.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Tension</strong> gives every chord an extension, allows deeper
			inversions, and lets a chord shrink to an eighth note.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h3>Keeping what you roll</h3>
	<p>
		Pin the chords you like and reroll the rest. Copy the chart as plain text,
		copy a link that opens the same progression, or export a MIDI file with the
		tempo already set. Everything runs in your browser, and your last idea is
		saved in local storage. See the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for
		the details.
	</p>
</InfoPage>
